A||||4||sqoach||Got new shocks and struts put in...||||11||
Z||000000||sqoach||10-12-2001||12:13 PM||||But now my front end sits a little higher. The back only went up slightly. Can't tell really. Both the old shocks and struts were crap. I compressed them and then wouldn't even un-compress. It rides much better and doesn't feel like a boat.<BR>I was thinking about cutting 1 coil from the front springs. I've heard good and bad about this. And I know this has been discussed a bunch. If I didn't use a torch, wouldn't it be ok? Just hacksaw a coil off. Don't use any heat. I would buy a set of lowering springs, but that's extra $ that I don't want to spend.<P>------------------<BR>-Adam<BR><FONT COLOR="BLUE">sqoach@yahoo.com</FONT><BR>            <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sqoach.gif">         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">            <BR>^^^^^^<B>new project</B>^^^^^^<BR><LI><FONT SIZE="1">84 SE (FOR SALE)*SOLD*<BR><LI>85 Coupe (parts car)($50)*sale pending*<BR><LI>86 SC</FONT>||||reg||11
Z||000001||Banzai||10-12-2001||12:40 PM||||I just pulled the stock springs off my 84'. If you want them(so you can experiment) I'll let em go for $25 plus shipping. They need paint or plastidip though.<P>Banzai !!!!!!!!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000002||Rick 88||10-12-2001||02:28 PM||||Try cutting a set of softer springs from a base car. I did this on my 88 GT and the car rides smooth. Your spring rate will increase when you cut it. Starting off with a softer spring offset the rate increase. <P>Rick 88||||reg||3
Z||000003||qwikgta||10-12-2001||11:07 PM||||I got a 4 1/2 inch grinder at Home depot for about $45.00 i have used it for many things, the best thing is that you can remove the grinding wheel and put on a 4 1/2 inch cut off wheel and cut through that spring in about 10 seconds, I know, I did it last weekend.  I cut one and a third coil off the front and got it about an inch and a half lower.  Got about an inch in the rear with a coil and a quarter.  Good luck<P>Rob<P>PS - it rides like a dream<P>------------------<BR>qwikgta@yahoo.com<BR>88 T-Top coupe, 66K, Header no Cat w/ Supertrapp, 16" GTZ rims, short shifter, GT rear swaybar, open element air cleaner, hood vents, clear tails, KYB's, dropped springs.<BR>http://www.homestead.com/robsfieroproject/robsfieroproject.html   <BR>http://www.vafieros.com<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigpic2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||MrPBody||10-13-2001||01:43 PM||||Yep, new gas-pressurized shocks/struts raised my ride height, too.<P>I cut front and rear springs on my '85 SE using a die grinder and cutoff disk -- one coil from front; 1/2 coil from rear.<P>Cutting springs will stiffen the ride, so -- if you have WS6 suspension -- I second the advice to find some softer-rate salvage springs, and cut them instead.<P>Since you have the front suspension apart, install Moog adjustable upper ball joints, too.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
